            0.086
          ],
          "content": "The World of Numbers"
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "bbox": [
            0.855,
            0.069,
            0.884,
            0.084
          ],
          "content": "45"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.114,
            0.11,
            0.643,
            0.132
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "3.3 Integers: Expanding the Horizon"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.143,
            0.885,
            0.202
          ],
          "content": "Brahmagupta did not stop at zero. He realised that if subtraction of a number from itself can result in zero (5 – 5 = 0), then what would happen if we subtracted a larger number from a smaller one $(3 - 5 =  口 ) ?$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.202,
            0.885,
            0.24
          ],
          "content": "To answer this, Brahmagupta grounded his mathematics in the reality of commerce and life."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.241,
            0.432,
            0.259
          ],
          "content": "He recognised two states:"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.142,
            0.262,
            0.883,
            0.282
          ],
          "content": "**Fortunes (Dhana):** Positive numbers, representing wealth or assets."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.142,
            0.286,
            0.758,
            0.307
          ],
          "content": "**Debts (Ṛiṇa):** Negative numbers, representing debts."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.314,
            0.886,
            0.412
          ],
          "content": "By moving to the left of zero on the number line, Brahmagupta formally introduced **Negative Numbers** to the world. The combination of positive natural numbers, their negative counterparts, and zero creates the set of **Integers**, denoted by the symbol Z (from the German word Zahlen, meaning numbers)."
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.134,
            0.428,
            0.863,
            0.524
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [
            {
              "bbox": [
                0.465,
                0.535,
                0.534,
                0.552
              ],
              "content": "Fig. 3.2"
            }
          ],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_4_image_9.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.113,
            0.587,
            0.521,
            0.608
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "3.3.1 The Arithmetic of Integers"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.616,
            0.885,
            0.657
          ],
          "content": "Brahmagupta gave explicit rules for adding and multiplying these integers, which we still use exactly as he wrote them over 1,300 years ago:"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.131,
            0.665,
            0.695,
            0.685
          ],
          "content": "1. **A fortune plus a fortune is a fortune:** 5 + 4 = 9."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.131,
            0.693,
            0.884,
            0.734
          ],
          "content": "2. **A debt plus a debt is a debt:** (– 5) + (– 4) = –9. (If you owe \\`5 and borrow \\`4 more, you owe \\`9.)"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.131,
            0.743,
            0.884,
            0.783
          ],
          "content": "3. **A fortune minus zero is a fortune, a debt minus zero is a debt:** $7 - 0 = 7, \\mathrm{and} - 6 - 0 = - 6.$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.131,
            0.787,
            0.883,
            0.828
          ],
          "content": "4. **The product of a debt and a fortune is a debt:** $(-3) \\times 4 = -12$ (If you take on 4 debts of \\`3, your total debt is \\`12.)"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.131,
            0.833,
            0.776,
            0.854
          ],
          "content": "5. **The product of two debts is a fortune:** $( - 3 ) \\times ( - 4 ) = 12$"
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
